  1843	\section{The Heisenberg group and Diophantine equations over the ring of integers}\label{sec:Heis}
  1844	
  1845	  \begin{dfn}
  1846	    The \textit{Heisenberg group} \(H(\mathbb{Z})\) is the class \(2\)
  1847	    nilpotent group defined by the presentation
  1848	    \[
  1849	      H(\mathbb{Z}) = \langle a, \ b, \ c \mid c = [a, \ b], \  [a, \ c] =
  1850	      [b, \ c] = 1 \rangle.
  1851	    \]
  1852	    The generator \(c\) is redundant, however we include it as it is usually
  1853	    easier to work with the generating set \(\{a, \ b, \ c\}\) than \(\{a, \
  1854	    b\}\).
  1855	
  1856	    The \textit{Mal'cev generating set} for the Heisenberg group is the set
  1857	    \(\{a, \ b, \ c\}\).
  1858	  \end{dfn}
  1859	
  1860	  \begin{dfn}
  1861	    The \textit{Mal'cev normal form} for the Heisenberg group is the normal
  1862	    form that maps an element \(g \in H(\mathbb{Z})\) to the unique word of
  1863	    the form \(a^i b^j c^k\), where \(i, \ j, \ k \in \mathbb{Z}\), that
  1864	    represents \(g\).
  1865	  \end{dfn}
  1866	
  1867	  The fact that this is a (unique) normal form for \(H(\mathbb{Z})\) is
  1868	  well-known (see, for example \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro}).
  1869	
  1870	  Whilst it is undecidable whether a system of equations in the Heisenberg group
  1871	  admits a solution \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro}, there is an algorithm that
  1872	  determines whether a single equation admits a solution
  1873	  \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro}. Even in class \(2\) nilpotent groups in general,
  1874	  the satisfiability of single equations is undecidable \cite{Romankov2016}, so
  1875	  single equations in the Heisenberg group are close to the `boundary' of
  1876	  decidability. It remains open whether the solution language to a single
  1877	  equation in the Heisenberg group is EDT0L. In the one-variable case, however,
  1878	  there is a positive answer:
  1879	
  1880	  \begin{theorem}[\cite{EDT0L_heisenberg}]
  1881	    \label{Heisenberg_eqns_EDT0L_thm}
  1882	    Let \(L\) be the solution language to a single equation with one
  1883	    variable in the Heisenberg group, with respect to the Mal'cev generating
  1884	    set and normal form. Then
  1885	    \begin{enumerate}
  1886	      \item The language \(L\) is EDT0L;
  1887	      \item An EDT0L system for \(L\) is constructible in \(\ns(
  1888	      n^8(\log n)^2)\).
  1889	    \end{enumerate}
  1890	  \end{theorem}
  1891	
  1892	  The proof of Theorem \ref{Heisenberg_eqns_EDT0L_thm} starts by reducing
  1893	  the question to two-variable quadratic equations in the ring of integers.
  1894	  The majority of the proof is then to show that these equations have EDT0L
  1895	  solution languages.
  1896	%\lcc{Alex, can you actually write down (state) a quadratic equation below? It will stand out more, and then you can refer to that equation when you mention \(\mathcal{E}\) in the proof.}
  1897	  \begin{theorem}
  1898	    \label{quad_eqn_thm}
  1899	    The solution language to a two-variable quadratic equation \(\mathcal E\):
  1900	    \[
  1901	    \alpha X^2 +
  1902	    \beta XY + \gamma Y^2 + \delta X + \epsilon Y + \zeta = 0
  1903	    \]
  1904	    in integers is
  1905	    EDT0L, accepted by an EDT0L
  1906	    system that is constructible in \(\ns(
  1907	    n^4 \log n)\), with the input size taken to be \(\max(|\alpha|, |\beta|,
  1908	    |\gamma|, |\delta|, |\epsilon|, |\zeta|)\).
  1909	  \end{theorem}
  1910	
  1911	  Proving Theorem \ref{quad_eqn_thm} uses a method of Lagrange to reduce
  1912	  arbitrary two-variable quadratic equations to \textit{Pell's equation}, which is
  1913	  an equation in the ring of integers of the form
  1914	  \[
  1915	    X^2 - DY^2 = 1,
  1916	  \]
  1917	  where \(X\) and \(Y\) are variables, and \(D > 0\) is not a perfect square.
  1918	  We insist that \(D > 0\) as if \(D \leq 0\), this has finitely many solutions,
  1919	  and we insist \(D\) is not a perfect square, as in this case we can factorise
  1920	  the equation to show that we can again only have finitely many solutions.
  1921	  To do this, it must first be reduced to the \textit{generalised Pell's
  1922	  equation}, which is of the form
  1923	  \[
  1924	    X^2 - DY^2 = N,
  1925	  \]
  1926	  where \(N \in \mathbb{Z}\). Lagrange's method shows that the set of
  1927	  solutions to an arbitary two-variable quadratic equation \(\mathcal E\)
  1928	  in integers is equal to
  1929	  \[
  1930	    \left\{ \left. \left(\frac{\alpha x + \beta y + \gamma}{\delta}, \
  1931	      \frac{x - \lambda}{\mu} \right) \ \right| \
  1932	    (x, \ y) \text{ is a solution to } X^2 - DY^2 = N \right\},
  1933	  \]
  1934	  where \(N, \ D \in \mathbb{Z}\) are computable from \(\mathcal{E}\). The
  1935	  proof that solutions to Pell's equation are EDT0L is straightforward, and
  1936	  we include the proof below. This can be easily modified for the generalised
  1937	  Pell's equation. What is more difficult is showing that the linear combinations
  1938	  of the solutions (that is, integers of the form \(\alpha x + \beta y + \gamma\)
  1939	  for \(\alpha, \ \beta, \ \gamma \in \mathbb{Z}\)) form an EDT0L language.
  1940	  The `division' part of the reduction is done using a construction of the
  1941	  EDT0L system, and does not require number theoretic methods.
  1942	
  1943	  We give the proof that the solutions to any Pell's equation form an EDT0L
  1944	  language below.
  1945	
  1946	  \begin{lem}
  1947	    Let \(X^2 - DY^2 = 1\) be an instance of Pell's equation, where
  1948	    \(D \in \mathbb{Z}_{> 0}\) is not a perfect square. Then
  1949	    \[
  1950	      \{a^x \# a^y \mid x^2 - Dy^2 = 1\}
  1951	    \]
  1952	    is an EDT0L language over the alphabet \(\{a, \ a^{-1}, \ \#\}\).
  1953	  \end{lem}
  1954	
  1955	  \begin{proof}
  1956	    The solutions to Pell's equation \(X^2 - DY^2 = 1\) take the form
  1957	    \[
  1958	      \{(x_n, \ y_n) \mid n \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\},
  1959	    \]
  1960	    where \((x_0, \ y_0) = (1, \ 0)\), \((x_1, \ y_1)\) is the smallest
  1961	    solution to \(X^2 - DY^2 = 1\) excepting \((1, \ 0)\), and \((x_n, \ y_n)\)
  1962	    for \(n > 1\) is recursively defined by
  1963	    \[
  1964	      x_n = x_1 x_{n - 1} + D y_1 y_{n - 1}, \quad y_n = y_1 x_{n - 1} +
  1965	      x_1 y_{n - 1}.
  1966	    \]
  1967	    This fact is well-known (see for example Chapter 3 of \cite{quad_dioph_eqns_book}). There has been a significant
  1968	    amount of work that uses different methods to compute \((x_1, \ y_1)\) with
  1969	    varying degrees of efficiency, however, for the purposes of showing that
  1970	    the language is EDT0L, we do not need to compute this. To construct the
  1971	    EDT0L system we would, and showing that the space complexity in which the EDT0L
  1972	    system can be constructed is nice would require one of these methods. First
  1973	    note that it suffices to show that the set of non-negative solutions is
  1974	    EDT0L. To see this, we will show that
  1975	    \[
  1976	      L = \{a^x \# b^y \mid x, \ y \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}, \ x^2 - Dy^2 = 1\}
  1977	    \]
  1978	    is EDT0L. Then note that \((x, \ y)\) is a solution if and only if \((x, \
  1979	    -y)\) is a solution if and only if \((-x, \ y)\) is a solution if and only
  1980	    if \((-x, \ -y)\) is.
  1981	%    \lcc{It is confusing here to talk about monoid morphisms while using inverses ... so should we do A, B, or some other approach?}
  1982	    Thus by applying a free monoid homomorphism to map
  1983	    \((a, \ b)\) to any of \((a, \ a)\), \((a^{-1}, \ a)\), \((a, \ a^{-1})\)
  1984	    and \((a^{-1}, \ a^{-1})\), we can show that the set of solutions lying in
  1985	    any quadrant is EDT0L, using Theorem \ref{EDT0L_closure_properties_thm}. We
  1986	    can then use Theorem \ref{EDT0L_closure_properties_thm} to show that the
  1987	    union of these languages is EDT0L. As this is the set of all solutions,
  1988	    this completes the proof.
  1989	
  1990	    We now define our EDT0L system \(L\). Our extended alphabet will be
  1991	    \(C = \{a, \ b, \ \#, \ a_x, \ a_y, \ b_x, \ b_y\}\) and our start word will
  1992	    be \(a_x \# b_x\). Define \(\varphi \in \End(C^\ast)\) by
  1993	    \begin{align*}
  1994	      a_x \varphi & = a_x^{x_1} a_y^{D y_1}
  1995	      & b_x \varphi = b_x^{x_1} b_y^{D y_1} & \\
  1996	      a_y \varphi & = a_x^{y_1} a_y^{x_1}
  1997	      & b_y \varphi = b_x^{y_1} b_y^{x_1}. &
  1998	    \end{align*}
  1999	    Then define \(\theta \in \End(C^\ast)\) by
  2000	    \begin{align*}
  2001	      a_x \varphi & = a
  2002	      & b_x \varphi = \varepsilon \\
  2003	      a_y \varphi & = \varepsilon
  2004	      & b_y \varphi = b.
  2005	    \end{align*}
  2006	
  2007	    Our rational control is \(\varphi^\ast \theta\) (see Figure
  2008	    \ref{Pell_eqn_fig}). By construction, if \(n \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\),
  2009	    then \((a_x \# b_x) \varphi^n\) will be of the form \(u \# v\),
  2010	    where \(u\) contains precisely
  2011	    \(x_n\) occurrences of \(a_x\) and \(y_n\) occurrences of \(a_y\), and
  2012	    \(v\) contains precisely \(x_n\) occurrences of \(b_x\) and \(y_n\) of
  2013	    \(b_y\). Thus \((a_x \# b_x) \varphi^n \theta = a^{x_n} \# b^{y_n}\),
  2014	    and we have shown that our EDT0L system accepts \(L\).
  2015	
