   147	\section{Introduction}
   148	
   149	The aim of this survey is to show how formal languages can be used to describe
   150	solutions to equations in groups. Equations are a generalisation of two of
   151	Dehn's decision problems for groups: the word problem and the conjugacy problem.
   152	An \textit{equation} in a group \(G\) is an identity \(w = 1\), where \(w\) is a
   153	word over \(G\) together with a finite set of \textit{variables} and their
   154	inverses. A \textit{solution} to an equation is an assignment of an element of
   155	\(G\) to each variable, such that plugging this into \(w\) yields a word
   156	equivalent to \(1\). Thus the conjugacy problem in a group \(G\) can be thought
   157	of as solving the equation \(X^{-1} g X h^{-1} = 1\) for all \(g, \ h \in G\). Since the word and conjugacy problems are undecidable in general, solving equations in arbitrary groups will also be undecidable, and so an important question is for which classes of groups solving equations can be successful.
   158	
   159	Finding algorithms to decide whether or not
   160	equations in a variety of different classes of groups and monoids admit solutions has been an active area of research since the 1950s. The first
   161	major positive results are due to Makanin, in the 1970s, when he
   162	proved that it is decidable whether a finite system of equations in a free group or free monoid
   163	is satisfiable \cites{Makanin_systems, Makanin_semigroups, Makanin_eqns_free_group}. Since then, Makanin's work has been extended to
   164	show the decidability of the satisfiability of equations in hyperbolic
   165	\cites{rips_sela, dahmani_guirardel} and certain relatively hyperbolic groups \cite{dahmani}, solvable Baumslag-Solitar groups
   166	\cite{diophantine_metabelian_grps}, right-angled Artin groups \cite{DiekertMuscholl},
   167	and more.
   168	
   169	Whilst Makanin's work can determine if an equation admits a solution, it does
   170	not describe the set of solutions. Razborov later created a method that allows
   171	one to construct the solutions to systems of equations in a free group
   172	\cites{Razborov_thesis, Razborov_english}. Since sets of solutions are
   173	often infinite, there are multiple ways they can be represented, if at all. One
   174	method in which solutions can be described is by expressing the set of solutions
   175	as a language, and then defining a grammar or a machine generating the language. A formal language is any set of words over a finite alphabet, and one of the standard ways to categorise languages is the Chomsky hierarchy, which classifies them in terms of complexity into regular, context-free, context-sensitive and recursively enumerable. Since context-free languages are not able to describe these sets (see Section \ref{sec:sol_lang}) and context-sensitve are too general to capture the structure of the solutions in an optimal way, a different class is needed; this class is the focus of the survey.
   176	
   177	\textbf{EDT0L languages and solutions.} In 2016, the first author, Diekert and Elder successfully employed formal languages to describe
   178	the set of solutions to systems of equations in free groups
   179	\cite{eqns_free_grps}. The class of languages used was the class of EDT0L
   180	languages. Diekert and Elder generalised this to virtually free groups
   181	\cite{DEijac}, and Diekert, Je\.{z} and Kufleitner extended it to right-angled
   182	Artin groups \cite{EDT0L_RAAGs}. Hyperbolic groups \cites{eqns_hyp_grps, eqns_hyp_grps_conf}, virtually abelian groups \cite{VAEP} and virtually direct
   183	products of hyperbolic groups \cite{EDT0L_extensions} followed later.
   184	
   185	In the 1960s, Lindemayer introduced a collection of classes of languages called
   186	\textit{L-systems}, which were originally used for the study of growth of
   187	organisms. EDT0L (\textbf{E}xtended \textbf{D}eterministic
   188	\textbf{T}able \textbf{0}-interaction \textbf{L}indenmayer) languages are one of the L-systems, and were introduced by
   189	Rozenberg in 1973 \cite{ET0L_EDT0L_def_article}. L-systems, including EDT0L
   190	languages, were studied intensively in computer science in the 1970s
   191	and early 1980s; the most studied families of L-systems are subclasses of indexed languages, which although not historically part of the Chomsky hierarchy, fit nicely between context-free and context-sensitive (see Figure \ref{L_system_containments_intro_fig}). A description of solution sets as EDT0L languages
   192	was known before only for quadratic word equations over a free monoid by \cite{FerteMarinSenizergues14}. Since the first author, Diekert and Elder's use of EDT0L languages to
   193	study equations in free groups, a number of other works have used EDT0L
   194	languages (or a similar class called ET0L languages)
   195	\cites{bishop_elder_journal, EDT0L_permuations, appl_L_systems_GT} to describe sets in groups other than solutions to equations. ET0L
   196	languages generalise both EDT0L and context-free languages. We omit
   197	the definition here, as they are never used directly, although we
   198	will refer to them
   199	(see \cite{math_theory_L_systems} for the definition).
   200	%Figure \ref{L_system_containments_intro_fig} shows
   201	%how EDT0L and ET0L languages fit into the Chomsky hierarchy of languages.
   202	
   203	When writing the solutions as EDT0L languages, an intrinsic part is to set up the convention on how to write these, as well as establish a way of expressing group elements in terms of words. Suppose $G$ is generated by a finite set $X$ and a normal form $\eta$, that is, a uniform way of expressing elements as words over $X$, is fixed (see Definition \ref{def_NF}).
   204	Our overarching convention (with more flexibility in the case of virtually abelian groups) is that for an equation on $n$ variables in $G$, any solution $(g_1, \dots, g_n)$, with $g_i \in G$, will be converted into a tuple of normal forms $(g_1\eta, \dots, g_n\eta)$, and then into a single word $(g_1 \eta) \# \cdots \# (g_n \eta)$ over the alphabet $X \cup \{\# \}$, where $\#$ is a symbol not in $X$. Writing the solutions in terms of a natural normal form, and not as arbitrary words representing group elements, is one of aspects that makes the algorithms and results about equations challenging. We collect in Table \ref{table:results_free_hyp} the first results obtained in this area, in loosely chronological order.
   205	
   206	\begin{figure}
   207	  \begin{tikzpicture}
   208	    [scale=.6, auto=left]
   209	
   210	    \node (reg) at (0, 0) {Regular};
   211	    \node (EDT0L) at (5, 2.5) {EDT0L};
   212	    \node (CF) at (5, -2.5) {Context-free};
   213	    \node (ET0L) at (10, 0) {ET0L};
   214	    \node (IND) at (14, 0) {Indexed};
   215	     \node (CS) at (20, 0) {Context-sensitive};
   216	
