   298	
   299	\textbf{Free and hyperbolic groups.} The results in Table \ref{table:results_free_hyp} all refer to hyperbolic
   300	groups, and these are indeed the groups where decidability of solving equations
   301	was first established, by Makanin for free groups
   302	\cites{Makanin_systems, Makanin_semigroups, Makanin_eqns_free_group}, then by Rips and Sela for
   303	torsion-free hyperbolic \cite{rips_sela}, and by Dahmani and Guirardel \cite{dahmani_guirardel} for all hyperbolic groups.
   304	The reason why hyperbolic groups have been tackled before other classes is,
   305	roughly and intuitively, that the negative curvature helps set bounds on the
   306	number and sizes of paths relating to thin triangles in hyperbolic Cayley
   307	graphs: geodesic triangles are essential when solving systems of triangular
   308	equations, and any equation in a hyperbolic group is handled by transforming it
   309	into a system of triangular ones. Furthermore, hyperbolic groups are
   310	particularly suitable for expressing the solutions in terms of normal forms, as
   311	the most natural normal languages (all geodesics, quasi-geodesics, shortlex
   312	normal forms, etc) are regular for any generating set. The geometry of
   313	hyperbolic Cayley graphs, as well as the regularity of normal forms, were
   314	exploited by the first author and Elder to a large degree in their work.
   315	
   316	
   317	
   318	\textbf{Other classes of groups.} Outside the world of negative curvature, the main classes of groups considered have
   319	been (virtually) abelian and nilpotent. Whilst a lot can be done for virtually
   320	abelian groups in terms of both complexity of algorithms and formal languages
   321	(see Section \ref{sec:virtab}), the results for nilpotent groups are very
   322	limited because undecidability of solving systems of equations occurs already
   323	for all non-abelian free nilpotent groups \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro}. However,
   324	considering single one-variable equations in nilpotent groups such as the Heisenberg
   325	group turned out to be fruitful, and led to some beautiful connections to
   326	Diophantine equations of degree $2$ (see Section \ref{sec:Heis}). These results
   327	are collected in Table~\ref{table:results_other}.
   328	Even though the satisfiability of systems of equations in all free nilpotent groups is undecidable, for single equations there are some positive results.
   329	Duchin, Liang and
   330	Shapiro \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro} showed that the satisfiability of single
   331	equations in all class \(2\) nilpotent groups with a virtually cyclic
   332	commutator subgroup (including all Heisenberg groups) is decidable. Roman'kov
   333	showed that this is not the case for all class \(2\) nilpotent groups
   334	\cite{Romankov2016}. For groups with a higher nilpotency class, the
   335	undecidability of solving even single equations occurs in free nilpotent groups
   336	of class \(3\) and sufficiently large rank \cite{DuchinLiangShapiro}.
