   476	
   477	\textbf{Constraints.} The addition of constraints, that is, requiring the solutions to belong to specific sets, was also considered when showing solution
   478	languages were EDT0L. Adding rational constraints allows one to restrict
   479	specific variables to lie in certain sets, such as finitely generated
   480	subgroups or conjugacy classes. Recognisable constraints are more
   481	restrictive, but can be used to enforce that solutions lie in cosets
   482	of finite index subgroups. In any class of groups where solution
   483	languages to systems of equations are EDT0L, the same can be said for
   484	systems of equations with recognisable constraints \cite{EDT0L_extensions}.
   485	Rational constraints pose more challenges, however.
   486	
   487	Systems of equations with rational constraints were shown to have EDT0L
   488	solution langauges in free \cite{eqns_free_grps} and virtually free groups
   489	\cite{DEijac}. For hyperbolic groups, the addition of a slightly more
   490	restrictive class of constraints called quasi-isometrically embedded rational
   491	constraints were shown to preserve the property of having EDT0L solutions when
   492	added to systems of equations \cite{eqns_hyp_grps}. Rational constraints can
   493	also be added to virtually abelian groups \cite{EDT0L_extensions}. For other
   494	classes of groups, this may be difficult. For example, in the Heisenberg group
   495	it remains open as to whether there is an algorithm to check if a given element lies
   496	in a given rational set.
   497	
   498	%\newpage
   499	\tableofcontents
   500	
   900	  representative. We will often abuse notation and treat a
   901	  normal form \(\eta\) as a subset \(\im \eta \subseteq (\Sigma^\pm)^\ast\), rather
   902	  than as a function, as this is sometimes more convenient.
   903	
   904	We give some standard examples of normal forms.
   905	\begin{ex} \
   906	\begin{enumerate}
   907	    \item The standard normal form for $\mathbb{Z}^2$ on generators $\{a, b\}$ is the set $\{a^nb^m \mid m,n \in \mathbb{Z}\}$ over $\{a, a^{-1}, b, b^{-1}\}$. This normal form is geodesic and regular.
   908	    \item The standard normal form for the free group $F(a,b)$ on generators $\{a, b\}$ is the set of reduced words over $\{a, a^{-1}, b, b^{-1}\}$. This is geodesic and regular, as shown in Example \ref{ex:reglang} (2).
   909	    \item More generally, the set of shortlex representatives of a hyperbolic group $G$ over a generating set endowed with an order is a geodesic and regular normal form (see \cite{groups_langs_aut} for details).
   910	    \item One of the standard normal forms used for elements in the Baumslag-Solitar groups $\BS(1,k)$ is given in Lemma~\ref{lem:BSnormform}. This normal form is regular but not geodesic.
   911	\end{enumerate}
   912	\end{ex}
   913	
   914	  We can now express solutions as languages, with respect to
   915	  a given normal form.
   916	
   917	  \begin{dfn}
   918	    Let \(G\) be a group with generating set \(\Sigma\),
   919	    and let \(\eta \colon G \to (\Sigma^\pm)^\ast\) be a normal form for \(G\)
   920	    with respect to \(\Sigma\). Let \(\mathcal{E}\) be a system of equations in
   921	    \(G\) with a set \(\mathcal{S}\) of solutions. The \textit{solution
   922	    language} to \(\mathcal{E}\) is the language
   923	    \[
   924	      \{(g_1 \eta) \# \cdots \# (g_n \eta) \mid (g_1, \ \ldots, \ g_n) \in
   925	      \mathcal{S}\}
   926	    \]
   927	    over \(\Sigma^\pm \sqcup \{\#\}\).
   928	  \end{dfn}
   929	
   930	\begin{rmk}
   931	Note that context-free languages do not in general work for describing solutions to equations when using the above convention. Even in
   932	\(\mathbb{Z}\), the system $\{X = Y, Y = Z\}$ has the solution
   933	language \(\{a^x \# a^x \# a^x \mid x \in \mathbb{Z}\}\) with respect to the
   934	standard normal form, and this is not a context-free language.
   935	
   936	\end{rmk}
   937	
   938	\subsection{Space complexity}
   939	  We briefly define space complexity. A more comprehensive introduction to complexity, including the standard big O notation $\Oh$, can be
   940	  found in \cite{computational_compl}.
   941	
   942	  \begin{dfn}
   943	    Let \(f \colon \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\) be a function.
   944	    We say that an algorithm runs in \(\mathsf{NSPACE}(f)\) if it can be
   945	    performed by a non-deterministic Turing machine that satisfies the
   946	    following:
   947	    \begin{enumerate}
   948	      \item A read-only input tape;
   949	      \item A write-only output tape;
   950	      \item A read-write work tape such that a computation path in the Turing
   951	        machine with input length \(n\) uses at most \(\mathcal{O}(nf)\) units
   952	        of the work tape.
   953	    \end{enumerate}
   954	
   955	    An algorithm is said to run in \textit{non-deterministic linear (resp.
   956	    quadratic, resp. polynomial) space} if it runs in \(\mathsf{NSPACE}(f)\),
   957	    for some linear (resp. quadratic, resp. polynomial) function \(f \colon
   958	    \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\). %As with big O notation, we will write usually write \(\ns((n)f)\), rather than \(\ns(f)\).
   959	  \end{dfn}
   960	
   961	  % We will use this definition to show that we can construct the multivariable
   962	  % finite-state automaton from Theorem \ref{theorem:VA_n_reg}, and hence the
   963	  % EDT0L system from Corollary \ref{VA_EDT0L_cor}, in non-deterministic
   964	  % quadratic space. Recall that a multivariable finite-state automaton has a
   965	  % set of vertices, edges, an assignment of labels to edges, a specified start
   966	  % state, and a set of accept states that all must be constructed.
   967	
   968	  \begin{rmk}
   969	    \label{constructible_rmk}
   970	    We will often say that grammars or automata that define languages are
   971	    \textit{constructible} in \(\ns(f)\). This means that there is an algorithm
   972	    that runs in \(\ns(f)\), which takes an input that will be specified (which
   973	    is sometimes other grammars or automata), and outputs the desired grammar or
   974	    automaton.
   975	  \end{rmk}
   976	
   977	\subsection{Equations with constraints}
   978	
   979	A \textit{finite system of equations with rational (recognisable)
   980			constraints} \(\mathcal{E}\) in a group \(G\) is a finite system of
   981			equations \(\mathcal{F}\) with variables \(X_1, \ \ldots, \ X_n\),
   982			together with a tuple of rational (recognisable) subsets \(R_1, \ \ldots, \
   983			R_n\) of \(G\). A \textit{solution} to \(\mathcal{E}\) is a solution
   984			\(\phi\) to \(\mathcal{F}\), such that \(X_i \phi \in R_i\) for all \(i\).
   985	
   986	
   987	 We cover here the basic definitions of rational and recognisable subsets of
   988	  monoids. Both types are used as constraints for variables in equations in
   989	  groups, and we will use recognisable constraints to show that the class of
   990	  groups where solutions to systems of equations form EDT0L languages is closed
   991	  under passing to finite index subgroups. Whilst our main focus will be
   992	  rational subsets of groups, rational subsets of monoids are
   993	  required in the definition of an EDT0L language.
   994	
   995	  %
   996	  % Recall that a \textit{language} over \(\Sigma\) is any subset of
   997	  % \(\Sigma^\ast\), where \(\Sigma\) is a finite set, called an
   998	  % \textit{alphabet}. Recall also that a \textit{regular} language is any
   999	  % language accepted by a finite-state automaton. We refer the reader to
  1000	  % \cite{groups_langs_aut}, Chapter 2, for further details on languages and finite
  1001	  % state automata.
  1002	
  1003	% \lcc{Alex, can you also add rational and recognisable sets in groups? It's a bit confusing that you only mention monoids.}
  1004	  \begin{dfn}
  1005	    Let \(S\) be a monoid (for example, a group), and \(\Sigma\) be a (monoid)
  1006	    generating set for \(S\). Define \(\pi \colon \Sigma^\ast \to S\) to be the
  1007	    natural homomorphism. We say a subset \(A \subseteq S\) is
  1008	    \begin{enumerate}
  1009	      \item \textit{recognisable} if \(A \pi^{-1}\) is a regular language over
  1010	      \(\Sigma\);
  1011	      \item \textit{rational} if there is a regular language \(L\) over
  1012	      \(\Sigma\), such that \(A = L \pi\).
  1013	    \end{enumerate}
  1014	    Note that this definition is independent of the choice of generating set.
  1015	  \end{dfn}
  1016	
  1017	  \begin{rmk}
  1018	    Recognisable sets are rational.
  1019	  \end{rmk}
  1020	
  1021	 % We give a few examples of recognisable and rational sets.
  1022	
  1023	  \begin{ex}
  1024	    Finite subsets of any monoid are rational. Finite subsets of a group \(G\)
  1025	    are recognisable if and only if \(G\) is finite \cite{herbst_thomas}. Finite
  1026	    index subgroups of any group are recognisable, and hence rational.
  1027	  \end{ex}
  1028	
  1029	  % The following result of Grunschlag relates the rational subsets
  1030	  % of a finite index subgroup of a group \(G\) to the rational subsets of \(G\)
  1031	  % itself.
  1032	
  1033	  % \begin{lem}[\cite{Grunschlag_thesis}, Corollary 2.3.8]
  1034	  %   \label{Grunschlag_rational_lem}
  1035	  %   Let \(G\) be a group with finite generating set \(\Sigma\), and \(H\) be a
