  1727	\noindent\textbf{Lifting $\Phi$ to the free group $F(S)$.}
  1728	Let $b$ be a constant that depends on $\delta$ and linearly on $q$ as in \cite{dahmani}. We run in lexicographic order through all possible tuples of words $$\mathbf c=(c_{11},c_{12},c_{13},\dots, c_{q1},c_{q2},c_{q3})$$ with $c_{ji}\in S^*$ and $|c_{ji}|_S\leq b$.
  1729	
  1730	 Then for each tuple $\mathbf c$ we use Dehn's algorithm to check whether $c_{j1}c_{j2}c_{j3}=_G1$; if this holds for all $ j $ we construct a system $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$ of equations of the form
  1731	\begin{equation}\label{csystem}
  1732	X_j=P_jc_{j1}Q_j, \ Y_j=Q_j^{-1}c_{j2}R_j, \ Z_j=P_jc_{j3}R_j, \ \ 1\leq j\leq q,
  1733	\end{equation}
  1734	where $P_i, Q_i, Z_i$ are new variables. This new system has size $\Oh(n^2)$.
  1735	 In order to avoid an exponential size complexity we write down each system $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$ one at a time, so the space required for this step is $\Oh(n^2)$.
  1736	Let $\mathcal{Y}\supset \mathcal X$, $|\mathcal{Y}|=m$, be the new set of variables, including all the $P_i, Q_i, Z_i$.
  1737	
  1738	Note that any solution to  $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$
  1739	in the free group $F(S)$ is clearly a solution to $\Phi$ in the original hyperbolic group $G$ when restricting to the original variables $\mathcal X$. That is, if $(w_1, \dots, w_m) \subseteq F^m(S)$ is a solution to $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$, then $((w_1)\pi, \dots, (w_k)\pi)$ is a solution to $\Phi$ in $G$. This shows the soundness of the algorithm.
  1740	
  1741	
  1742	Now let $\lambda_G \geq 1, \mu_G\geq 0$ be the constants provided by \cite[Proposition 3.4]{dahmani}.
  1743	
  1744	%Note that if a word $w\in S^*$ is a $(G,S,\lambda_G,\mu_G)$-\qgeod\ and satisfies $w=_G1$ then $|w|_S \leq \mu_G$. We can construct an NFA $\mathcal N$ which accepts all words in $S^*$ equal to $1$ in the hyperbolic group $G$ of length at most $\mu_G$  in constant space (using for example Dehn's algorithm), and in our next step we will use this rational constraint to handle
  1745	%the variable $X_N$ added in Step 1 (to remove inequalities).
  1746	
  1747	\medskip
  1748	
  1749	\noindent \textbf{Solving equations with constraints in $F(S)$.}
  1750	We now run  the algorithm for solving free group equations with rational constraints from \cite{eqns_free_grps} (which we refer to as tCDE algorithm) which takes input $\Phi_\mathbf{c}$ of size $\Oh(n^2)$, plus for each $Y\in \mathcal Y$ the rational constraint that the solution for  $Y$ is a $(G,S,\lambda_G, \mu_G)$-quasi-geodesic.
  1751	Since these constraints have constant size (depending only on $G$, not the system $\Phi$),  they do not contribute to the $\Oh(n^2)$ size of the input to the CDE algorithm.
  1752	
  1753	
  1754	We modify the CDE algorithm to ensure every node printed by the algorithm includes the additional label $\mathbf c$. (This ensures the FSA we print for each system $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$ is distinct.)
  1755	This does not affect the complexity since $\mathbf c$ has size in $\Oh(n^2)$. Let $\mathcal C$ be the set of all tuples ${\mathbf c}$.
  1756	We run the modified CDE algorithm described above  (adding label $\mathbf c$ for each state printed) to print an FSA (possibly empty) for each $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$, which is the rational control for an EDT0L grammar that produces all solutions as freely reduced words in $F(S)$, which correspond to solutions as $(G,S,\lambda_G,\mu_G)$-quasi-geodesics to the same system $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$ in the hyperbolic group.
  1757	
  1758	
  1759	Note that if $(g_1,\dots, g_k)\in  G^k$ is a solution to $\Phi$ in the original hyperbolic group,  \cite{rips_sela}
  1760	guarantees that there exist canonical representatives $w_i$ that are $(G,S,\lambda_G, \mu_G)$-quasi-geodesics with $w_i=_Gg_i$, which have freely reduced forms $u_i=_Gw_i$ for $1\leq i\leq m$, and our construction is guaranteed to capture any such collection of words. More specifically, if $(w_1,\dots, w_r)$ is a solution in canonical representatives to $\Phi$
  1761	then $(u_1,\dots, u_k,\dots u_{|\mathcal Y|})$  will be included in the solution to $ \Phi_{\mathbf c}$ produced by the CDE algorithm, with $u_i$ the reduced forms of $w_i$ for $1\leq i\leq m$. This shows completeness once we union the grammars from all systems $\Phi_{\mathbf c}$ together.
  1762	
  1763	
  1764	To produce the solutions in $G$, we add a new start state with edges to each of the start states of the FSAs with label $\mathbf c$, for all $\mathbf c \in \mathcal C$. We obtain an automaton which is the rational control for the language $L$ of solutions to the input equation. This rational control makes $L$ an EDT0L language of $(G, S, \lambda, \mu)$-quasi-geodesics, where $\lambda:=\lambda_G, \mu:=\mu_G$.
  1765	The space needed is exactly that of the CDE algorithm for free groups on input $\Oh(n^2)$, which is $\NSPACE(n^2\log n)$.
  1766	
  1767	Further steps are required to produce the solutions in terms of shortlex representatives (see \cite[Theorem 7.4(2)]{eqns_hyp_grps}), but these do not affect the language or space complexity.
