  2070	\section{Closure properties}\label{sec:closure}
  2071	
  2072	  We summarise the group theoretic constructions that preserve the
  2073	  property of having EDT0L solution languages to systems of equations. It
  2074	  remains open whether this property is stable under many other
  2075	  constructions, such as passing to finite index overgroups.
  2076	
  2077	  \begin{theorem}[\cite{EDT0L_extensions}, Theorem
  2078	  A]
  2079	    Let \(G\) and \(H\) be groups where solution languages to systems of
  2080	    equations are EDT0L, with respect to normal forms \(\eta_G\) and
  2081	    \(\eta_H\), respectively, and these EDT0L systems are constructible in
  2082	    \(\ns(f)\), for some \(f\). Then in the following groups, solutions to
  2083	    systems of equations     are EDT0L, and an EDT0L system can be constructed
  2084	    in non-deterministic \(f\)-space:
  2085	    \begin{enumerate}
  2086	      \item \(G \wr F\), for any finite group \(F\);
  2087	      \item \(G \times H\);
  2088	      \item Any finite index subgroup of \(G\);
  2089	    \end{enumerate}
  2090	    In the following groups, solutions to systems of equations are EDT0L, and
  2091	    an EDT0L system can be constructed in \(\ns(n^4 \log n)\):
  2092	    \begin{enumerate}[resume]
  2093	      \item Any group that is virtually a direct product of hyperbolic
  2094	      groups;
  2095	      \item Dihedral Artin groups.
  2096	    \end{enumerate}
  2097	    If \(\eta_G\) and \(\eta_H\) are both quasigeodesic or regular, then the
  2098	    same will be true for the normal forms used in (1), (2) and (3). It is
  2099	    possible to choose normal forms for the groups that are virtually direct
  2100	    products of hyperbolic groups (4), and dihedral Artin groups in (5) that
  2101	    are regular and quasigeodesic.
  2102	  \end{theorem}
  2103	
  2104	  Proving (1) and (2) relies on a technical lemma about EDT0L languages where
  2105	  every word contains the same number occurrences of the letter \(\#\).
  2106	  It shows that such EDT0L systems can be generated using a well-behaved EDT0L
  2107	  system, that allows two such languages to be `concatenated in parallel', as
  2108	  is required for direct product constructions. We must first formally
  2109	  define the property that such EDT0L systems will have to allow for
  2110	  concatenation in parallel.
  2111	
  2112	  \begin{dfn}
  2113	    Let \(\Sigma\) be an alphabet, and \(\#_1, \ \ldots, \ \#_n \in \Sigma\). A
  2114	    \textit{\((\#_1, \ \ldots, \ \#_n)\)-separated EDT0L system} is an EDT0L
  2115	    system \(\mathcal{H}\), with a start word of the form \(\omega_0 \#_1
  2116	    \omega_1 \#_2 \cdots \#_n \omega_n\), where \(\omega_i \in (\Sigma
  2117	    \backslash \{\#_1, \ \ldots, \ \#_n\})^\ast\) for all \(i\), and such that
  2118	    \(\#_i \phi^{-1} = \{\#_i\}\) for all \(i\) and every \(\phi\) in the
  2119	    rational control.
  2120	  \end{dfn}
  2121	
  2122	  \begin{lem}[\cite{EDT0L_extensions}, Lemma 3.4]
  2123	    \label{n_hash_sep_lem}
  2124	    Let \(L\) be an EDT0L language, such that every word in \(L\) contains
  2125	    precisely \(n\) occurrences of the letter \(\#\), where \(n \in
  2126	    \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\). Let \(f \colon \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \to
  2127	    \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\). Then
  2128	    \begin{enumerate}
  2129	      \item There is a \((\#, \ \ldots, \ \#)\)-separated EDT0L system
  2130	      \(\mathcal H\) that accepts \(L\).
  2131	      \item If an EDT0L system for \(L\) is constructible in \(\ns(f)\), then
  2132	      \(\mathcal H\) is constructible in \(\ns(f)\).
  2133	    \end{enumerate}
  2134	  \end{lem}
  2135	
  2136	  Using Lemma \ref{n_hash_sep_lem}, it is straightforward to prove the
  2137	  following. This result shows the fact that direct products preserve the
  2138	  property of having EDT0L solution languages to systems of equations. Wreath
  2139	  products with finite groups require a little extra work; see
  2140	  \cite{EDT0L_extensions} for details.
  2141	
  2142	    \begin{lem}[\cite{EDT0L_extensions}, Lemma 3.5]
  2143	    \label{intermesh_EDT0L_lem}
  2144	    Let \(L\) and \(M\) be EDT0L languages, such that every word in \(L \cup M\)
  2145	    contains precisely \(n\) occurrences of the letter \(\#\). Let \(f \colon
  2146	    \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\). Then
  2147	    \begin{enumerate}
  2148	      \item The language
  2149	      \[
  2150	        N = \{u_0 v_0 \# \cdots \# u_n v_n \ | \ u_0 \# \cdots
  2151	        \# u_n \in L, \ v_0 \# \cdots \# v_n \in M\},
  2152	      \]
  2153	      is EDT0L;
  2154	      \item If EDT0L systems for \(L\) and \(M\) are constructible in
  2155	      \(\ns(f)\), then an EDT0L system for \(N\) is constructible in \(\ns(f)\).
  2156	    \end{enumerate}
  2157	  \end{lem}
  2158	
  2159	  Before we discuss the proof of the fact that finite index subgroups (of finitely generated groups) also
  2160	  preserve the property of having EDT0L solution languages, we must first
  2161	  define the generating set and normal form. By Schreier's Lemma, we know that a (finite) generating set can be explicitly constructed, and this
  2162	  generating set induces a normal form. We give the definition of Schreier generators below,
  2163	  but refer the reader to \cite{EDT0L_extensions} for a proof.
  2164	
  2165	  \begin{dfn}
  2166	    Let \(G\) be a group, generated by a finite set \(\Sigma\), \(H\) be a
  2167	    finite index subgroup of \(G\), and \(T\) be a right transversal of \(H\) in
  2168	    \(G\). For each \(g \in G\), let \(\bar{g}\) be the (unique) element of
  2169	    \(T\) that lies in the coset \(Hg\). The \textit{Schreier generating set}
  2170	    for \(H\), with respect to \(T\) and \(\Sigma\), is defined to be
  2171	    \[
  2172	      Z = \{tx \overline{tx}^{-1} \mid t \in T, \ x \in \Sigma\}.
  2173	    \]
  2174	  \end{dfn}
  2175	
  2176	  Given a normal form for a given group with a given finite generating
  2177	  set, the proof of Schreier's Lemma also constructs an explicit normal
  2178	  form for the finite index subgroup, using the normal form of the
  2179	  original group. We give the normal form below. Passing to this normal
  2180	  form preserves the properties of being regular and quasigeodesic.
  2181	
  2182	  \begin{dfn}
  2183	    \label{Schreier_norm_form_def}
  2184	    Let \(G\) be a group, generated by a finite set \(\Sigma\), \(H\) be a
  2185	    finite index subgroup of \(G\), and \(T\) be a right transversal of \(H\)
  2186	    in \(G\).  Let \(Z\) be the Schreier generating set for \(H\). Fix a normal
  2187	    form \(\eta\) for \((G, \ \Sigma)\).
  2188	
  2189	    We define the \textit{Schreier normal form} \(\zeta\) for \((H, \ Z)\),
  2190	    with respect to \(\eta\), as follows. Let \(h \in H\), and suppose \(h \eta
  2191	    = a_1 \cdots a_n\), where \(a_1, \ \ldots, \ a_n \in \Sigma^\pm\). Let
  2192	    \(t_0\) be the unique element of \(T \cap H\), and define \(t_i =
  2193	    \overline{a_1 \cdots a_i}\). Define \(h \zeta\) by
  2194	    \begin{equation}
  2195	      \label{Schreier_nrom_form_eqn}
  2196	      h \zeta = (t_0 a_1 \overline{t_0 a_1}^{-1}) (t_1 a_2 \overline{t_1
  2197	      a_2}^{-1}) \cdots (t_{n - 1} a_n \overline{t_{n - 1}a_n}^{-1}).
  2198	    \end{equation}
  2199	    The fact that this indeed defines an element of \(H\), and equals \(h\), is
  2200	   part of the proof of Schreier's Lemma.
  2201	  \end{dfn}
  2202	
  2203	  The proof of the following relies on indexing the letters in the extended
  2204	  alphabet with generators of \(G\) and elements of a fixed right
  2205	  transversal, to keep track of the coset our element lies in,
  2206	  to ensure we stay within the normal form.
  2207	
  2208	  \begin{proposition}[\cite{EDT0L_extensions},
  2209	  Proposition 6.3]
  2210	    \label{fin_index_EDT0L_prop}
  2211	    Let \(f \colon \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\). Let \(G\)
  2212	    be a group where solutions to systems of equations are EDT0L in \(\ns(f)\),
  2213	    with respect to a normal form \(\eta\). Then the same holds in any finite
  2214	    index subgroup of \(G\) with respect to the Schreier normal form, inherited
  2215	    from \(\eta\).
  2216	  \end{proposition}
  2217	
  2218	\section{Baumslag-Solitar groups}
  2219	  \label{sec:BS}
  2220	
  2221	Duncan, Evetts, Rees and Holt \cite{DuncanEvettsHoltRees} proved that various
  2222	equations in soluble Baumslag-Solitar groups have EDT0L languages with respect
  2223	to a standard normal form, including multiplication `triples' and centralisers
  2224	of fixed elements. A \textit{Baumslag-Solitar group} is a group defined by
  2225	the presentation
  2226	\[
  2227	  \text{BS}(j, \ k) = \langle a, \ b \mid b^{-1} a^j b = a^k \rangle,
  2228	\]
  2229	for some \(j, \ k \in \mathbb{Z} \backslash \{0\}\). These are soluble precisely
  2230	when \(j = 1\) or \(k = 1\), so we will (without loss of generality) assume \(j
  2231	= 1\). If \(k = \pm 1\), then this group is virtually abelian, and so need not be
  2232	considered here. In addition, the authors in \cite{DuncanEvettsHoltRees} assume
  2233	\(k > 1\), although they conjecture that the proofs will hold up when \(k <
  2234	-1\), modulo some technicalities.
  2235	
  2236	We now define the normal form that is used to show solution languages to certain
  2237	equations are EDT0L.
  2238	
  2239	\begin{lem}[\cite{DuncanEvettsHoltRees}, Section 3]
  2240	\label{lem:BSnormform}
  2241	  Every element in \(\text{BS}(1, \ k)\) is uniquely expressible in the form
  2242	  \[
  2243	    b^t \alpha^{i_m} b^{-1} \alpha^{i_m - 1} b^{-1} \cdots \alpha^{i_1} b^{-1}
  2244	    \alpha^s,
  2245	  \]
  2246	  where \(t \in \mathbb{Z}\), \(m, \ s \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\), \(i_j \in
  2247	  \{0, \ \ldots, \ k - 1\}\) for all \(j\), with \(i_m \neq 0\) whenever
  2248	  \(m > 0\).
  2249	\end{lem}
  2250	
  2251	\begin{theorem}[\cite{DuncanEvettsHoltRees}]
  2252	  Let \(G = B(1, \ k)\) for some \(k\). Then, all with respect to the
  2253	  normal form in Lemma \ref{lem:BSnormform},
  2254	  \begin{enumerate}
  2255	    \item The solution language to \(XY = Z\) is EDT0L;
  2256	    \item The solution language to \(XYZ = 1\) is EDT0L;
  2257	    \item If \(g \in G\), then the solution language to \(Xg = gX\) is EDT0L.
  2258	  \end{enumerate}
  2259	\end{theorem}
  2260	
  2261	With respect to this normal form, Duncan, Evetts, Holt and Rees conjecture that
  2262	there exist equations with solution languages that are not EDT0L. This does not
  2263	necessarily mean that there will be other regular normal forms for which all
  2264	solutions will be EDT0L; however, since a group with a decidable Diophantine
  2265	problem, but non-EDT0L solution languages with respect to any `sensible' normal
  2266	form has yet to be found, this would be significant progress in using EDT0L
  2267	languages to understand solutions to equations. They conjecture that the
  2268	solution language to the equation
  2269	\[
  2270	 XY = YX
  2271	\]
  2272	is not EDT0L. By intersecting this solution language with the regular
  2273	language \(b^\ast a \# b^\ast a^\ast\), showing that this solution
  2274	language is not EDT0L is reduced to showing that the language
  2275	\[
  2276	  \{b^r a \# b^{rn}a^\frac{k^{rn} - 1}{k^r - 1} \mid r, \ n \geq 0\}
  2277	\]
  2278	is not EDT0L.
  2279	
